                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 94.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Departure of President Prado.</hi></head>
                <p rendition="#center">[From the South Pacific Times.]</p>
                <p>His Excellency President Prado, came from Lima, accompanied by several of his
                    ministers, by the train which left Desamparados Station at 3.5 p.m. on Thursday
                    last. Arriving at Callao, he at once proceeded on board the Pacific Steam
                    Navigation Company’s steamer Payta, which left that afternoon on her regular
                    itinerary for Panama. The following morning two documents, of which the
                    following are translations, were published:</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Mariano Ignacio Prado, Constitutional President of the
                    Republic.</p>
                <p>Inasmuch as I am authorized to leave the country by a legislative resolution of
                    May 2, 1879, and very urgent and important matters demanding my presence abroad,
                    and it being my duty and desire to do all I can in favor of the country,</p>
                <p>I decree:</p>
                <p>Solely. That the first vice-president take charge of the presidency of the
                    republic in conformity with articles 90 and 93 of the constitution.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">Given in the government house in
                            <placeName>Lima</placeName> on <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-12-18">the 18th day of December, 1879</date>.</dateline>
                    <lb/><signed><list>
                            <item>MARIANO I. PRADO.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Manuel G. de La Cotera</hi>.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">B. Elguera</hi>.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Adolfo Quiroga</hi>.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. M. Quimper.</hi></item>
                        </list></signed></closer>
